-- sprint unmask-arg-tag-dispatch S-5 — enumeration cell "an interface default whose
-- body calls a sibling", licensed by the contract's §7 criterion 5 and named in S-5's
-- packet §4.2/§4.6. S-4 (#2032) narrowed the local pin so a generalized local with NO
-- enclosing rigid scope now dispatches through the arg-tag chain; this is the untested
-- shape S-1's own report flagged as one of three reasons an arm can't be emitted
-- ("the inner same-interface method needs `requires` dicts").
--
-- `Greet`'s default `hello` calls the SAME interface's `name` method on the same
-- receiver — the default body needs a `requires Greet a` dict for that inner call,
-- not just the outer dispatch dict.
--
-- Ground truth, derived from the source, NOT captured:
--   * `Dog`'s impl defines only `name`, so it inherits the DEFAULT `hello`, whose body
--     is `"hi " ++ name x`; at `x = Dog`, `name Dog` resolves to `Dog`'s own `name`
--     impl ("dog"), giving "hi dog".
--   * `Cat`'s impl overrides `hello` directly ("meow-hello"), so its `name` impl is
--     never reached through `hello` at all.
--   * `f`'s two calls, in source order: "hi dog" then "meow-hello".
--
-- Measured (not enshrined blind): `check`/`run`/`build`+binary all agree at
-- `hi dog|meow-hello` on this tree — this is a CLEAN accept, not a divergence; the
-- `requires`-dict gap S-1 flagged does not (at this shape, both receivers are DATA
-- heads, so the arg-tag chain can still discriminate and route to a dict-passing
-- default arm). See S-5's report for the primitive-head sibling question, deliberately
-- not asked here since it compounds two open residuals in one fixture.
--
-- Verdict: ACCEPT.
interface Greet a where
  hello : a -> String
  hello x = "hi " ++ name x
  name : a -> String

data Dog = Dog

data Cat = Cat

impl Greet Dog where
  name _ = "dog"

impl Greet Cat where
  name _ = "cat"
  hello _ = "meow-hello"

main =
  let f = v => hello v
  println (f Dog ++ "|" ++ f Cat)
